WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:07.440
Atop Earth’s highest mountain above sea
level hardly seems like an easy place to live.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.820
Mount Everest’s tall peaks, freezing temperatures,
fierce winds, and ever-present snow make it

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.349
hard to support life.

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:19.020
And while it’s true that very few plants
or animals can survive there, the most famous

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.630
resident certainly seems at home—the Yeti.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:29.190
The huge, hairy monster has been spotted walking
across Asian mountains from China to the Himalayas.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:34.030
Some say it looks like an ape, others a bear,
and others even insist it’s an unidentified

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.120
humanoid species.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.040
The first reports of a “wild man” in the
Himalayas in the Western word appear during the

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.380
19th century&nbsp;– but didn’t really get much attention.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.810
But in the 20th century a few expeditions,
some reported tracks, and one very famous

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.720
photograph began a crazed hunt for the Yeti.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:57.000
[Monstrum intro]
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka, and this is Monstrum.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.480
It’s generally believed Yeti is a mispronunciation
of the word yeh teh from Tibetan Sherpa dialect,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.430
meaning either “cliff dwelling bear” or
“animal of rocky places.”

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:12.640
Regardless, when you hear “yeti” you probably
picture the huge creature with fluffy white

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.540
fur that pops up in movies all the time —the
one commonly called the “Abominable Snowman”.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.280
"Abominable! Can you believe that?
Do I look abominable to you?"

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.180
But there’s no real folklore that supports
that version before the 20th century.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:33.159
Written accounts of the Tibetan legend of
the yeti date back to the 12th century.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.590
Also called a “wild man,” many
accounts describe the creature as an ape-like

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:43.820
being, over six feet tall walking on its back legs
with reddish-brown fur covering its muscular body.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:49.560
Explanations range from a real animal displaced
in search of food, to a purely fictional folklore

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:50.780
creature.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.700
Tibetan tradition recognizes a variety of
yeti.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:57.670
One said to walk on two legs,
and another larger yeti

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.280
that preys on livestock, and walks on four legs.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.160
Yeti are believed to live in the alpine forests
below the snow line, although they will brave

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.270
higher altitude for food and shelter.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.870
They don’t eat or hunt humans (although
they may steal food from them), preferring

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.510
the mosses, frogs, and pikas that live on
the mountain.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:23.380
In many stories, the yeti is nocturnal and
emits a high-pitched screech or whistle; in

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:28.340
Tibetan tradition, it is more common to hear
a yeti then to see one.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:35.080
The two-legged meh teh appears in Buddhist
temple decorations and religious painted scrolls.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.240
According to a religious and historical Tibetan
text from the 12th century

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.200
these “wild people” and humans come from
shared ancestors, although the meh teh is

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.020
neither fully human nor fully animal.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.700
This is why much of the art in Tibetan temples
shows the “yeti realm” of rebirth between

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.010
humans and animals.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:59.360
And Tibetan Buddhist stories feature yetis
as both kind and malevolent characters.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.940
One popular tale from the Himalayas tells
of an injured yogi helping a “wild man”

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.150
with an infected foot from a splinter.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.450
The yogi kindly removes the splinter and cleans
the wound.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:14.320
The grateful yeti thanks the yogi by bringing
him a tiger, which the man skins and offers

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.200
to a monastery.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.480
Legends are often born when we blur the lines
between fiction and real life events.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.740
In the 17th-century, a Buddhist religious
leader [Lama Sangwa Dorje] walked from India

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.390
to Nepal to reside in a cave as a religious
hermit.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:32.920
While living there, he claimed friendly yetis
would bring him food and water, allowing him

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.840
a life of quiet meditation.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:41.140
When one of the yetis died, he kept the scalp
as a holy relic in the temple he built in 1667.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.380
This real-life relic became a part of blessing
rituals for the temple monks.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:50.989
Later, a yeti hand was added to the collection,
another tribute to the creatures that made

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:52.709
the temple possible.

00:03:52.709 --> 00:03:58.200
The temple monks refused to allow anyone to
remove the relics for hundreds of years, but

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:04.280
in 1959, explorer Peter Byrne stole a few
finger bones from the hand, substituting them

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.980
with human ones.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:11.409
So, yeti legends and stories were culturally
important long before the Western world was

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:13.940
even aware of their existence.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:18.269
In reality, the yeti entered into Western
thought as a footnote.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.160
Literally, a footnote in a book.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:26.440
In 1832 British naturalist Brian H. Hodgson
writing about mammals in Nepal,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:32.180
noted that no monkeys could be found in the
northern and central regions, BUT a footnote

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:37.210
mentioned that during one expedition frightened
local shooters fled what they believed to

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.889
be a “wild man.”

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:44.129
He was told the creature moved upright, was
covered with long dark hair, and had no tail.

00:04:44.129 --> 00:04:48.349
Still no monkeys, but the tale of the curious
humanoid gave Hodgson pause.

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:54.069
In 1889, the first mention of yeti footprints
appeared in “Among the Himalayas”, by

00:04:54.069 --> 00:04:56.669
British explorer Laurence A Waddel.

00:04:56.669 --> 00:05:01.259
He writes that according to Tibetans the large
tracks were made by what they identified as

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:05.990
“hairy wild men who are believed to live
amongst the eternal snows.”

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:10.669
At the turn of the 20th Century, the first
exploration and hunting parties descended

00:05:10.669 --> 00:05:14.640
on the Himalayas in search of the mysterious,
hairy “wild man.”

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.260
But it wasn’t until Lieutenant Colonel Charles
Howard-Bury’s 1921 Mt. Everest exploration

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.860
that the press started to pay attention.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:28.740
Howard-Bury and five others saw tracks in
the snow that they could not identify. Local

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:34.060
laborers accompanying them identified the tracks
as belonging to “the Wild Man of the Snows."

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:38.980
Howard-Bury believed the prints in the snow
were from a grey wolf, but when he and his

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:43.360
team were interviewed by journalist Henry
Newman, the reporter found no excitement in

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.889
that explanation and instead focused on the
Tibetan “boogey man” with his backward

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:50.440
facing feet and long matted hair.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.960
He mistranslated Metoh-Kangmi, which roughly
means “man-bear snow-man,” as “filthy

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.559
snow-man” which he decided sounded better
as “abominable snowman.”

00:06:00.559 --> 00:06:05.370
Something about the name captured the public’s
imagination and interest in the creature snowballed.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:10.009
But expeditions to search for the yeti became
difficult because of political and religious

00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:14.700
tensions after China invaded Tibet, leading
to thousands of deaths.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.960
Until in 1951, an agreement was made affirming
China’s sovereignty over Tibet but allowing

00:06:19.969 --> 00:06:25.289
their political system to remain unchanged
and religious freedom to stay protected--including

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:27.400
the belief in the the meh teh.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:33.260
With a more stable political climate, explorers
once again poured in to hunt for the monster. Most

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:39.700
notable was the 1951 Mount Everest Reconnaissance
Expedition led by Eric Shipton, which produced

00:06:39.719 --> 00:06:45.809
the single most famous piece of yeti evidence—a
photograph of a footprint.

00:06:45.809 --> 00:06:50.309
Dubbed the “Shipton print,” the widely
published photo shows a huge human-like footprint

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:54.969
in the snow reportedly discovered on Mount
Everest at 18,000 feet.

00:06:54.969 --> 00:06:56.860
It was printed everywhere.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.840
Newspapers, science journals, and popular
magazines around the world plastered it across

00:07:01.849 --> 00:07:03.719
their pages.

00:07:03.719 --> 00:07:09.649
In 1952, a year after Shipton’s famous photograph,
a Swiss exploration team found possible Yeti

00:07:09.649 --> 00:07:11.879
tracks at 16,000 feet.

00:07:11.879 --> 00:07:16.669
A porter even claimed to be attacked by a
Yeti... buttt the rest of the team didn’t

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:18.169
corroborate his story.

00:07:18.169 --> 00:07:23.839
The monster search craze reached such a level
that the Nepalese government, seeing an opportunity

00:07:23.839 --> 00:07:29.080
to exploit Yeti, began offering special
Yeti hunting licenses.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:34.209
In 1954 the popular British newspaper The
Daily Mail funded a Himalayan Yeti-finding

00:07:34.209 --> 00:07:35.689
expedition.

00:07:35.689 --> 00:07:40.610
Texas oilman and monster hunter Tom Slick
also threw his cowboy hat into the ring.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:44.069
His bio included hunts for Bigfoot and the
Loch Ness Monster.

00:07:44.069 --> 00:07:48.949
In the 1950’s, he dedicated three expeditions
to the yeti, and Life Magazine offered him

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:53.480
$25,000 for the rights to any photos he took
of the creature.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.539
After 1959, tensions in the area came to a
head again, and a Tibetan uprising led to

00:07:58.539 --> 00:08:03.110
a Chinese crack-down on Yeti expeditions for
fear of espionage.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:08.119
For a decade, it was difficult to travel into
the country to look for the Yeti, but two

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.700
expeditions managed to wiggle through the
cracks.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:17.420
Between 1960 and 1961 Edmund Hillary and Marlin
Perkins led expeditions with a team of scientists

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:23.060
who examined the infamous yeti relic hand,
and ID’ed it as coming from a modern human,

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:27.110
although at the time they didn’t know Byrne
had swapped out the finger bones.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:33.800
Shockingly, the monks at the temple also allowed
them to take the famous yeti scalp—but only

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.019
if one of their elders could accompany it,
using the opportunity to raise money to build

00:08:38.019 --> 00:08:39.919
schools in Nepal.

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:45.192
In 1960, separate examinations of the scalp
in London, Chicago, and Paris found that is

00:08:45.192 --> 00:08:48.210
was a fake relic made from serow flesh.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:53.080
The discovery appeared to dampen the spirits
of yeti hunters and expeditions began to wane.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:58.000
None of the expeditions were able to bring
back conclusive evidence of a Yeti.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.460
Enter Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer, the
1964 Christmas television special, whose

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:08.160
fluffy white, arguably adorable yeti 
reignited the popularity.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.600
The fanged, white, furry character with blue
hands and feet at first seems scary, until

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.639
it’s revealed that it’s only suffering
from a toothache.

00:09:16.639 --> 00:09:20.860
The character, Bumble, is explicitly called
an “abominable snow monster.”

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:26.860
It’s my opinion this is the thing that created
the white-haired snow monster Westerners identify

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.990
as a yeti, the one most often seen in pop
culture today.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:36.240
In the 70s, the Chinese government sent more
than 100 people to investigate the Yeti.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.920
After they found nothing, they even allowed
Western expeditions into Tibet again but those

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.319
didn’t reveal anything either.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:48.080
In the same decade, the famous Shipton photograph
became widely viewed as a fake.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.660
One team member of the 1951expedition even
told a journalist that it was very possible

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.660
the photo was a hoax given that Shipton “definitely
liked to take the mickey out of people”

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:01.350
and “would think that was quite a good joke.”

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:05.689
But some of the expeditions over the years
did return with more than photos.

00:10:05.689 --> 00:10:11.060
Newly discovered fragments of bones, skin,
teeth, and fecal matter were all brought back

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:14.350
and investigated, but nothing conclusive developed.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:18.839
It seems that every time some new scientific
insight attempts to answer, “Is the yeti

00:10:18.839 --> 00:10:21.310
real?” the world pays attention.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.199
Take the Bhutan discovery for example.

00:10:23.199 --> 00:10:27.839
In 2001, a British zoologist collected
a few strands of hair from a hollow

00:10:27.839 --> 00:10:29.740
cedar tree in Bhutan.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:34.420
Under DNA analysis, the hair could not be
identified as coming from any animal known

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:35.420
to science.

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:40.259
Was this conclusive proof that some yet-unidentified
creature lived in the Himalayas?

00:10:40.259 --> 00:10:45.860
Not exactly. In 2014, yeti made the news again
when a study showed that the hair actually

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:49.540
came from an extinct paleolithic polar bear.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:55.890
In 2017, a team of scientists analyzed another
24 supposed yeti specimens taken from the

00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:01.930
Himalayas and Tibetan Plateau, including bone,
tooth, skin, hair, and fecal samples.

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:06.640
All but one of the samples (which came
from a dog) were from bears,

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:10.200
including Himalayan bears who
occupy the northwestern plateau.

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:15.620
Himalayan bears have paler, reddish-brown
fur, which seems awfully like the hair in

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.699
the descriptions of the original Tibetan yetis.

00:11:18.699 --> 00:11:24.050
Interestingly, the scientists discovered that
the Himalayan brown bear came from a unique

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:31.240
evolutionary lineage that diverged from all
other brown bears around 650,000 years ago.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:36.579
So, in their report, the scientists say that
the study’s findings “strongly suggest”

00:11:36.579 --> 00:11:40.139
that yeti’s are based on local brown and
black bears.

00:11:40.139 --> 00:11:41.970
I happen to agree.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:45.340
Bears also play a really important role in
Tibetan mythology and lore.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:50.060
There’s belief in a half-human and half-brown
bear creature called the mi-dred.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:55.490
In Upper Tibet among the Chinese Na-khi  people,
apes, marmots, brown bears, and mi-dred

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:58.480
are “the four brothers who are like humans
but are not human.”

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:03.810
They were believed to have the same ancestry
because they are all capable of standing on

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:05.440
their back legs.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:10.440
Folklore tells of brown bears abducting women
and producing children with them, and of brown

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:12.840
bears leaving human footprints.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:18.820
Tibetan folklore, religion, scientific discoveries,
there’s actually a lot going on with this

00:12:18.829 --> 00:12:20.730
monster—it’s kind of amazing.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:25.140
Both the snarling, bloodthirsty yeti
we see in some depictions

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:33.249
and the kinder, helpful versions we see in others
have some basis in Himalyan

00:12:33.249 --> 00:12:36.340
folklore, but ultimately they only touch the
surface.

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:41.140
The more dangerous representations promote
a healthy sense of wariness around bears,

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:45.779
or serve as warnings of the very real dangers
of the snow and high altitude.

00:12:45.779 --> 00:12:50.569
And the kinder, helpful versions of the yeti
speak to the Buddist belief that all living

00:12:50.569 --> 00:12:54.200
things have a purpose and a place in the world.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:13:00.480
Around 65,000 years ago.
6,500 thousand years ago. Right?

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.680
"650,000--"
-Holy Moly! 6,500,000!

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:12.280
"650,000."
Six-- [deep breath] 650,000. Wow.

